'''Whanganui''' (known as '''Wanganui''' until 1996) is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]]. It was first established in 1860 for the [[3rd New Zealand Parliament|3rd Parliament]], and has existed continuously since then.

It is held by [[Carl Bates]] of the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]], who won it in the [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023 general election]].

==Establishment==
In the 1860 electoral redistribution, the [[New Zealand House of Representatives|House of Representatives]] increased the number of representatives by 12, reflecting the immense population growth since the original electorates were established in 1853. The redistribution created 15 additional electorates, with between one and three members, and the {{NZ electorate link|Wanganui and Rangitikei}} electorate was split into two separate electorates: the {{NZ electorate link|Rangitikei}} electorate, and the Wanganui electorate, with one member each.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=32f}}

==Population centres==
{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Whanganui.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The current electorate is based on the urban area of [[Whanganui]], the town of [[Hāwera]], and smaller centres [[Kaponga]], [[Eltham]], [[Normanby, Taranaki|Normanby]], [[Manaia, Taranaki|Manaia]], [[Pātea]] and [[Waverley, Taranaki|Waverley]]; broadly speaking, the Whanganui and South Taranaki local government districts. The 2025 boundary review added the [[Ruapehu District]] towns of [[Raetihi]], [[Ohakune]], [[Waiouru]], [[National Park, New Zealand|Waimarino]] and [[Ōwhango]], and the [[Tongariro National Park]].<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=3 September 2025 |page=20 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

==History==
[[Henry Shafto Harrison]] was the first representative. His 7 February [[1860–1861 New Zealand general election|1861 election]] was declared invalid. He stood again in the {{By-election link|Wanganui|1861}} and was elected again. He then served the electorate until the end of the term in 1866.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=100}}{{sfn|Wilson|1985|p=203}}

Harrison, [[John Bryce]] and John Garner contested the {{NZ election link|1866}}. The nomination meeting was held on Friday, 2 March 1866. Harrison, Bryce and Garner received 51, 102 and 7 votes, respectively, at the election on the following day. Bryce was thus declared elected.<ref name="1866 Wanganui election">{{cite news|title=The Wanganui Election |url=http://paperspast.natlib.govt.nz/cgi-bin/paperspast?a=d&cl=search&d=WI18660306.2.12 |access-date=19 August 2010 |work=Wellington Independent |volume=XXI |issue=2331 |date=6 March 1866 |page=4}}</ref> Bryce resigned in 1867.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=81}}

The resulting 6 May {{By-election link|Wanganui|1867}} was won by Harrison, and he served until the dissolution of Parliament on 30 December 1870.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=100}}

Bryce was again elected in the 30 January {{NZ election link|1871}}. This time, he served three parliamentary terms until the dissolution in November 1881.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=81}}

Wanganui became a two-member electorate for the 12 January [[1875–1876 New Zealand general election|1876 election]]. Apart from Bryce, [[Julius Vogel]] was elected, who was later to become Premier. Vogel resigned on 9 September 1876.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|pp=142-143}}

The resulting 2 October {{By-election link|Wanganui|1876}} was won by William Fox, resulting in his second period of representation of a Whanganui-centred electorate. He served until the end of the term on 15 August 1879.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=94}}

Bryce and [[John Ballance]] won the {{NZ election link|1879}}. They both served until the end of the term, with Parliament being dissolved on 8 November 1881.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=77}}

For the {{NZ election link|1881}}, the electorate reverted to being represented by a single member. The election, held on 9 December, was won by [[William Hogg Watt]]. He served until the end of the term, with Parliament being dissolved on 27 June 1884.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=145}}

The {{NZ election link|1884}} was once again won by John Ballance. This time, he would serve in three successive Parliaments. He died in office on 27 April 1893 during the [[11th New Zealand Parliament|11th Parliament]].{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=77}} Ballance formed the [[New Zealand Liberal Party|Liberal Party]] after the {{NZ election link|1890}} and became its leader, and thus introduced party politics to New Zealand. The [[Liberal Government of New Zealand]] would last for 21 years and is the longest serving government in New Zealand's history.

Ballance's death triggered the {{By-election link|Wanganui|1893}}, which was held on 13 June and won by [[Archibald Willis]], who was re-elected at the {{NZ election link|1893}} a few months later.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=147}}

[[Gilbert Carson (politician)|Gilbert Carson]] was successful in the {{NZ election link|1896}}. He served one term.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=84}} He was succeeded by Willis in the {{NZ election link|1899}}, who served two more terms for the electorate.{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=147}}

[[James Thomas Hogan]] won the {{NZ election link|1905}}. He served two terms{{sfn|Scholefield|1925|p=103}} and was defeated in the {{NZ election link|1911}} by [[Bill Veitch]].

Veitch had a long career in the electorate, serving until 1935, when he defeated. He was initially an [[Independent politician|independent]], but joined the [[New Zealand Liberal Party|Liberal Party]] in 1925, and changed to the [[United Party (New Zealand)|United Party]] in 1928.

[[Joe Cotterill]] won the electorate in the {{NZ election link|1935}} for the [[New Zealand Labour Party|Labour Party]]. He also had a long career, retiring in 1960 from the Wanganui seat.

He was succeeded by his party colleague [[George Spooner]] in the {{NZ election link|1960}}, who served three terms and was defeated in {{NZ election link|1969}} by [[Bill Tolhurst]] from the [[New Zealand National Party|National Party]].

Tolhurst served one term and at the {{NZ election link|1972}}, the electorate returned to Labour. [[Russell Marshall]] served six terms and retired in 1990.

In the {{NZ election link|1990}}, [[Cam Campion]] secured the seat for National. He retired in 1993 and died two years after that.

The seat returned to Labour again, with [[Jill Pettis]] winning the {{NZ election link|1993}}. She was the first woman to represent Wanganui. She served four terms, until her defeat in the {{NZ election link|2005}} by [[Chester Borrows]] of the National Party.<ref>{{cite web
 | url = http://www.parliament.nz/en-NZ/MPP/MPs/MPs/1/c/5/50MP127281-Borrows-Chester.htm
 | title = Hon Chester Borrows
 | publisher = New Zealand Parliament
 | access-date = 18 March 2012
}}</ref> Pettis served an additional term as a [[List MP]] until 2008. Borrows announced in 2016 that he would not seek reelection at the [[2017 New Zealand general election|2017 general election]]<ref>{{cite news | first=Jo | last=Moir |url=http://www.stuff.co.nz/national/politics/86974130/National-MP-Chester-Borrows-wishes-he-spoke-out-more-as-12-years-in-politics-ends?cid=app-iPad |title=In hindsight National's Chester Borrows says he "could have had more fights" as an MP |date=29 November 2016 |work=Stuff.co.nz |access-date=27 September 2017}}</ref> and the seat was won by Harete Hipango, retaining it for the National Party. She however, was defeated after one term by Labour's [[Steph Lewis]].

Several members (Fox, Vogel and Ballance) became Premier. [[Terry Heffernan]] stood in the electorate five times for four parties, from {{NZ election link year|1981}} to {{NZ election link year|1993}}.

